 :selected-ownership
 {:identity "Axxium owns actor credential/session verification and identity lifecycle, not buffer consent. Its actual configured integration and secure binding to the owner are unproven. No caller-supplied actor, JWT alone, harness session ID, admin role or Sol context is evidence of current authorization."
  :resource-consent "The Emacs resource owner issues and revokes explicit, narrow actor-and-request-bound consent for a registered direct buffer under a trusted operator control path. It also owns the allowlist, exclusive-text proof, actual check/release/mutation and serialization. This is a resource-owner extension, not a general identity or policy kernel. A read grant is distinct from replace consent."
  :disclosure "GovOne remains the independent governed-content admission/release owner for reads. Its existing route/release vocabulary is not proof of editor-specific consent. An actual matching integration and any needed mapping or owner extension must be separately established; unavailable or mismatched means no disclosure."
  :runtime "Sol may carry only a fixed typed request and authenticated invocation binding from a qualified identity boundary to the owner; it may not mint consent, choose an evaluator, supply a fence, or rely on its permissive policy stub. Knoxx is not required on the selected path."
  :consumer "Oikos retains the reviewed closed operations and offline preconditions; it cannot confer authority or provide the native linearization point."
  :i3 "Window read and focus remain held pending the actual i3 owner atomic lifetime/cut compare-and-act. Two snapshots, an XID or an external mutex do not qualify it."}
 :proposed-contract
 {:ingress "A separately reviewed authenticated host channel must bind the actual Axxium-verified principal and session/credential generation to the invocation inside a trusted owner custody boundary. A typed opaque invocation handle is issued only by that boundary; untrusted clients cannot construct, replace, select or invoke owner internals. Specify peer authentication, channel binding, lifecycle, forgery resistance, and bootstrap in exact source before approval. No such bridge is found or assumed; no arbitrary eval, shell, command, raw IPC or dynamic function tool."
  :identity-freshness "Axxium verification is not atomic with Emacs. The selected safe contract requires all identity invalidations relevant to this integration to enter the same owner admission order before their revocation is acknowledged as effective for this capability. Existing Axxium revoke endpoints do not demonstrate that contract. Until an owner-reviewed, fail-closed integration covers every invalidation path, disconnection, and revalidation, deny; do not promise immediate Axxium-wide revocation. If that cannot be provided, owner-consent revocation can be qualified separately but actor-revocation ordering remains an explicit blocker."
  :fixture-only "First admit only new, explicitly operator-created, agent-owned direct buffers via object identity, never buffer names, using a reviewed whole-call exclusion mechanism. Refuse indirect/shared aliases, hooks, overlays, reentrancy and any resource whose exclusivity cannot be established and maintained through effect or release. No assumed predicate or ordinary mutex proves this."
  :grant "A trusted operator action creates an owner-held grant, with unguessable opaque reference and issuer/owner generation, verified actor and authenticated invocation binding, resource object registration plus epoch/incarnation/revision, exact operation and scope, byte range, exact replacement bytes for writes, contract identity, issue/expiry, and one-use ID. Copy/canonicalize immutable input; compare exact bytes, not just mutable object identity. Grant reference is not itself client authority; do not log replacement or secret material. Explicit approval is required for each write; a distinct explicit scoped allowlist/grant governs reads. Reject mismatch, ambiguity, stale facts, unsupported scope and expiry at owner boundary."
  :governance "For reads independently obtain actual current GovOne-required admission and enforce output bounds and allowed release destination. Bind its real decision/version and lifetime to the request and final release; do not infer an editor grant from a routing allow. If policy change cannot be ordered with release or the admission cannot be kept valid over the finite operation, hold release."
  :revocation "Owner consent revocation is a durable tombstone and applies to the bound grant; revocation ACK is issued only after durable append/commit and after entering the same serialized owner order as releases and writes. A failed persistence, unavailable store, incomplete recovery, concurrent owner, rollback, or lost authority denies new work and cannot acknowledge revocation. Previously issued grants must never revive after restart. Do not equate durable consent revocation with Axxium credential revocation or GovOne policy revocation."
  :storage "Use an existing owner-approved durable Clio/Receipt River persistence adapter only if it can prove single-writer fencing, durable commit, recovery and anti-rollback for this owner. No existing adapter has been shown to satisfy these requirements here; an append-only ledger alone is not an atomic lock or durable transaction. Bind a monotonic generation and consumed/revoked IDs to storage; require actual crash-consistent persistence semantics and owner custody. No new ledger, policy or session kernel. If the existing owner cannot supply these properties, retain deny and ask for exact owner/source permission rather than silently downgrading."
  :ordering "One exclusive owner serialization domain covers grant issuance, revocation admission and ACK, identity invalidation admission if qualified, grant consumption, exact final facts, native write, and read release. A completed revoke ACK before the relevant linearization point forbids effect/disclosure; concurrent operations have a single observable order; a revoke after completion cannot undo it. No asynchronous callback approval followed by an unlocked effect qualifies. Any callback or hook that may yield, reenter, change buffer/aliases or bypass this order must be prevented or causes denial."
  :write "Durably consume the exact one-use grant before the first possible mutation while holding the owner fence, then revalidate principal, consent, current buffer identity/revision, range and exclusion without opening an ordering gap before mutation. Durable consumption alone cannot atomically commit Emacs memory and durable storage. If failure occurs after consumption and before/during/after mutation or response, return indeterminate where applicable and never automatically retry. Denial before mutation may consume a grant. Existing delete-then-insert may partially mutate on error; do not describe it as all-or-nothing without a separately qualified native transactional mechanism."
  :read "Read content remains private to owner; final scope/identity/consent and qualified governed-release checks and output limit must be in the same finite owner ordering boundary as the actual first disclosure to the trusted next custody boundary. If transport buffering or later release is outside that boundary, specify and qualify its own fence; if not possible, hold. A callback called :release is not proof of release atomicity."
  :crash "Persisted revoke and consume survive only under the proven storage guarantees. Restart creates a fresh owner epoch and invalidates old invocation handles, registrations and unconsumed grants unless a reviewed recovery protocol explicitly proves safe reissuance. Lost replies, partial edits, and crashes after possible mutation are indeterminate: at-most-one admitted attempt per durable grant, not durable exactly-once effects or proof of completion. No automatic replay."}
